@*
    File metadata: the block under the `#` heading, describing the document as a
    whole.

    The same record as a chapter's and a different fact. A `##` block says whether
    that chapter is current; the block under the title says whether the file is,
    and the two are set independently — a file may be adopted with a chapter in it
    still a draft. Which is the whole reason this is a shape of its own rather than
    a chapter with a bigger heading.

    Where a reader meets it is the other half. Read in the body, the file's block
    is at the top of a document — the one place a reader has already scrolled past
    by the time they think to ask whether any of it is still current. So a surface
    with a header of its own draws it up there instead, around the name it
    describes, on the part of the pane that stays put: FileView does exactly that,
    and tells the read view underneath it to skip the fence it has taken over.
    Nothing here knows about a header; what it knows is that the heading it is
    handed is the file's name rather than a block in the document, and that
    changes what happens when there is no record.

    Two heading fragments, because the two cases need not be the same element.
    Inside a record the headline is a row — the name and the status align on it —
    so the host's name and detail lines go in as one wrapped item, or the details
    would sit beside the name and the status after both. With no record there is no
    row to be an item of, and the host's own markup goes in as it always was. One
    parameter would force a wrapper into the case that has nothing to wrap.

    A host is free to pass the same fragment to both, and FileView's header does:
    its heading carries the file's path as a second item, so it has to be a row in
    both cases, and with no record its own column is made the row instead. What
    stays true is that this component does not decide that for a caller — the
    wrapper is the host's, because what shares the row with the status is.

    Which is why an absent record is a parameter value here and not a caller's
    branch. `null`, and a block that states nothing, are the same answer — the
    file said nothing about itself — and the header still has to say which file it
    is. MetadataView renders no element at all for an empty record, by design, so a
    header that handed it the file's name would lose the name to a wrapper that
    stood down. Asking the question here means every host that draws a file's
    record gets that answer, rather than each of them remembering to check.
*@

@if (Metadata is { IsEmpty: false } record)
{
    <MetadataView Metadata="@record"
                  Vocabulary="@Vocabulary"
                  ShowFields="@ShowFields"
                  OnStatusChanged="@OnStatusChanged"
                  OnNavigate="@OnNavigate"
                  HrefFor="@HrefFor"
                  AriaLabel="@AriaLabel"
                  CssClass="@CssClass"
                  TestId="@TestId"
                  Heading="@Heading" />
}
else
{
    @HeadingAlone
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The file's own record, or nothing. Null and a block that states
    /// nothing are the same answer, and both draw <see cref="HeadingAlone"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public MetadataRecord? Metadata { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The file's name, as one item of the record's headline. Wrapped by
    /// the host: the headline is a row, and what shares that row with the status is
    /// the host's business.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? Heading { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The same name with no record around it, for a file that says
    /// nothing about itself. Separate from <see cref="Heading"/> because it is not
    /// in a row and needs no wrapper — and required in practice, since a header
    /// that drew nothing here would stop naming the file.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? HeadingAlone { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The status words this file's surface allows, which is what turns
    /// the status from a word into a value out of a list. See
    /// <see cref="MetadataView.Vocabulary"/>. A knowledge surface passes
    /// <c>KnowledgeStatus.Vocabulary(folder)</c>.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public MetadataStatusVocabulary Vocabulary { get; set; } = MetadataStatusVocabulary.None;

    /// <summary>Raised with the status a reader picked for the file. The file's
    /// own, and never a chapter's: a host that persists this writes the block under
    /// the title.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnStatusChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised with the reference a reader activated.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<KnowledgeReference> OnNavigate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Resolves a reference to a link target; null leaves that reference
    /// as text rather than a broken link.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<KnowledgeReference, string?>? HrefFor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether everything but the status is drawn. On by default; a header
    /// with room for one line turns it off and keeps the status.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowFields { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>The group's name for a screen reader. A file's record is worth
    /// naming for the file — several panes may be open at once — so a host passes
    /// the file's name in; the default is the record's own.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string AriaLabel { get; set; } = "Knowledge metadata";

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }
}
